Our unweighted mean difference estimates is the mean difference between groups of respondents, without considering the composition of the analytical sample. This means we gave each respondent equal weight. Said differently, the unweighted mean difference estimate targets the sample average treatment effect: the effect among the 682 respondents in the analytical sample.
The weighted regression that authors used for Table 2 takes account of the difference in sample composition and adjusts if the panel is skewed towards certain group. This helps the authors to give an accurate estimate describing the average treatment effect of the population, for instance US white evangelical Republicans.
Although the two estimates do not need to be exactly the same, a vast difference between them is worth looking into. The difference is more prominent if 1) the effects differ across groups that the weights adjust for, 2) the decision to respond is related to the outcome (ex. when deciding to respond to the questionnaire itself gives a bias), and 3) the weights are highly variable, making the weighted estimate less precise.

Answer #2.
The results in the paper suggest that religious-values message increases the feeling thermometer towards refugees in general and the support for refugee resettlement itself, while it does not positively affect the support for specific policies(ex. children of refugees studying in public schools / refugees receiving unemployment benefits), with no significant positive effect in either treatment. If anything, for the message+source cue treatment, there was a slight backlash on unemployment benefit provision for refugees.
This could be understood as follows. While the religious value frame may evoke warmer feelings on refugees and support for resettlements, it does not extend to policy supports. 
The authors mention that this might also be the case because the treatment does not mention government policies specifically, therefore if the religious value message mentioned certain programs on refugees in particular, the result may vary. Also, taking their political affiliation into account, the self-identified evangelical Republicans tend to be politically conservative and to favor small government. In this context, the support on refugees may be understood as support for a bigger government, which by itself the respondents would not support regardless of which group is better off by the policy. Instead, they may prefer to support refugees through non-governmental options like churches and non-profits, which may have been particularly salient in the condition with a religious source cue.

Answer #3.
The paper does not support the hypothesis (H2) that an evangelical source cue makes the religious values message more effective. The authors also mentioned that by comparing the two treatment conditions (RV vs. RV+SC), they did not find support from H2(p.2157).
Following are the comparisons mentioned, from Table 2.
Firstly, in the question on feeling thermometer, the point estimate shows that the message-only recipients felt warmer towards refugees than those who got it with the source cue.
(compared with control group, +11.39 for RV(p<0.01) vs. +7.18 for RV+SC(p<0.01))
Secondly, the mean of the support for refugee resettlement was slightly higher in message-only recipient group than in the message-source cue recipient group.
(+0.05 for RV (p<0.10) vs. +0.03 for RV+SC (not significant))
Lastly, on the question of unemployment benefits, the control group favored benefits more than message-with-source cue recipients by 3 percentage points, which may signify backlash.
(12% vs. 9%; −0.25 for RV+SC, p<0.05)
These three results show that at best the source cue did not strengthen the effect of religious value framing. 


Answer #4.
Theoretical claim: The paper claims that religious value framing can impact the political behavior of voters, even when it runs counter to their party’s position.
Specifically, a refugee-friendly religious value framing would shift self-identified white evangelical Republicans to feel warmer about refugees and be more supportive of refugee resettlement, with strong evangelical identifiers shifting more and strong Republican identifiers resisting.
Central empirical finding: The pre-registered survey experiment was conducted with 682 white evangelical Republicans. Participants were randomly given no text (control), a refugee-friendly religious value message and two reflective questions, or the same message with source cues. Those who received the message without a source cue felt about 11 points warmer about refugees (0–100 scale) and modestly more supportive of resettlement, but support for government benefits for refugees did not increase. Adding an evangelical source cue did not strengthen the effect. The effect was largest among strong evangelical identifiers but strong Republican identifiers also shifted in a pro-refugee direction..

Answer #5.
The same religious framing experiment could also be done for Korean evangelicals. Korean Evangelicals not only vary in their political affiliation but also in how important they believe religion is when making political decisions.

One of the periods when religion impacted political behavior was right after the martial law declaration of December 2024, until the impeachment ruling of 2025. Some pastors supported the martial law declaration on religious grounds, created peer pressure within churches to be more supportive of the martial law declaration. For Evangelical Progressives, this creates the same kind of conflict between religious and partisan identities that the original study examined.

The experiment measures whether a religious values frame that runs counter to their political position can change Korean Evangelical Progressives’ attitudes on the martial law declaration and the impeachment.

The religious framing would be different, mostly an excerpt from a pastor’s sermon during the service. Since some churches upload their services online (e.g., YouTube), it is possible to get excerpts from services during the period mentioned. The excerpt would emphasize the religious grounds these pastors gave, such as protecting Christian values in society. Excerpts containing unverified factual claims (e.g., election fraud) would be excluded. The text would be identical in the two treatment conditions, and only whether the source cue is included would differ.

Target Population: Korean Evangelical Progressives. These are respondents who self-identify as Protestant and as born-again or evangelical, and who support the Democratic Party of Korea or identify as progressive.


Treatment:

- Control: no text
- Treatment 1: Religious message supporting the martial law declaration and criticizing the acceptance of the impeachment ruling, with no source
Treatment 2: The same message as 2, with a source cue
e.g., The following is from a sermon by the senior pastor (with the name) of a large evangelical church (with the name) in Korea”

Outcome:

Political assessment of the martial law declaration: “The martial law declaration was understandable” (0-10 scale)
Feeling thermometer of the impeached president (Yoon Suk Yeol): feeling thermometer (0–100)
Support for the impeachment: acceptance of the Constitutional Court’s ruling (0-10 scale)

Strength of religious identity (how important religion is when making political decisions) and strength of partisan identity are measured pre-treatment as moderators.

Average treatment effect of interest:

ATE_1 = E[Y | Treatment 1] − E[Y | Control]
ATE_2 = E[Y | Treatment 2] − E[Y | Control]
Source cue effect = ATE_2 − ATE_1

On martial law declaration/impeachment:

Treatment 1 would be less understanding towards the martial law declaration and the political deeds of the impeached president compared to the control group.
Treatment 1 would be more favorable on the impeachment compared to the control group.
Treatment 2 would show similar results to Treatment 1, meaning the source cue effect would be close to zero.
The effects would be larger for those with stronger religious identity and smaller for those with stronger partisan identity.

One limitation is that the experiment would be fielded after the impeachment was concluded. 
Many respondents may have already heard similar messages and have firm opinions, which could mute the treatment effects.

```{r}
## 6.1 Unweighted ATE

outcomes <- c("refugee_thermometer", "resettlement_support", "school_support", "benefits_support")

means <- sapply(outcomes, function(y)
  tapply(demora[[y]], demora$treatment, mean, na.rm = TRUE))

ATE <- sweep(means, 2, means["Control", ])
ATE <- ATE[rownames(ATE) != "Control", ]

round(ATE, 3)
```

